## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement cost-aware, latency-conscious routing in a simple, auditable way. Focuses on per-tenant accounting, model pricing, and practical test coverage.

## Key Concepts

- Cost-aware routing and SLA mapping
- Per-tenant ledger and budget enforcement
- Simple fallback behaviors and test plans

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: ledger update and routing decision
function routePrompt(tenant, prompt) {
  const remaining = tenant.budget - tenant.spent;
  for (const m of ['fast','balanced','accurate']) {
    if (cost(m, prompt) <= remaining && latency(m, prompt) <= tenant.sla[m]) {
      tenant.spent += cost(m, prompt);
      return m;
    }
  }
  return 'cached';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle budget rollovers and monthly resets?
- How would you test this with A/B experiments across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cost- and latency-aware routing in multi-tenant LLM ops, including dynamic model selection and prompt segmentation under burst load.

## Key Concepts
- SLA-driven routing and per-tenant budgets
- Dynamic model variant selection (cost vs. latency)
- Prompt chunking strategies and batching
- Telemetry, drift detection, and resilience under burst traffic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: select a model variant based on tenant budget and latency target
function selectVariant(tenant, state, latencyTarget) {
  const budgetLeft = state[tenant].budget - state[tenant].spent;
  const withinLatency = state[tenant].avgLatency <= latencyTarget;
  if (budgetLeft > 0 && withinLatency) return 'highQuality';
  if (budgetLeft > 0) return 'balanced';
  return 'cheap';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure fairness when tenants with large budgets dominate capacity?
- How would you validate latency targets under sudden burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end design of a multi-tenant LLM-ops pipeline balancing latency, privacy, policy management, and observability. It assesses ability to design streaming prompts, per-tenant models, hot-updatable policies, drift alerts, and tests.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy engine
- Data locality and privacy controls
- Streaming prompt handling
- Dynamic model routing
- Drift detection and audits
- Tamper-evident logs and auditable trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: pick a model based on policy
function selectModel(tenant, policy) {
  const p = policy[tenant] || {};
  if (p.forceGuard) return 'safety-guarded';
  if (p.latencyBudget && p.latencyBudget < 75) return 'base';
  return p.preferredModel ?? 'base';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate drift triggers in production without constant retraining?
- What would be your approach to simulate hot policy updates during peak load?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware routing under mixed latency budgets and per-tenant policies. Requires integration of policy engines, redaction, quotas, data locality, auditability, and testing under burst traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy modeling
- Latency budgeting and SLA targets
- Model variant routing and cost-speed tradeoffs
- Redaction at prompt and response with data locality
- Tamper-evident auditing and drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo routing decision example
function decideRoute(ctx) {
  const tenant = ctx.tenant
  const policy = policyStore[tenant] || policyStore['default']
  // Evaluate latency and redact flags
  const model = policy.chooseModel(ctx.prompt)
  const redact = policy.shouldRedact(ctx.prompt)
  return { model, redact }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test latency budgets under burst traffic and verify cross-region consistency?
- How would you implement policy drift detection and safe rollback without service disruption?
